@*
    The facet panel: one plain GET form, no script.

    The querystring *is* the state, so a filtered listing is linkable, the back button works, and the
    server recomputes every count on every request. Nothing here is a control that remembers
    something — there is no state anywhere but the URL, which is what makes a shared link the same
    page for the person who receives it.

    Every count beside a value came from the same pass that produced the listing below it (see
    FacetedSearch), so a choice cannot promise results it will not deliver, and a value nothing
    matches is never drawn at all.
*@

<form method="get" action="/games" role="search" class="facet-form">
    <fieldset class="facets">
        <legend>filters</legend>

        <label for="q" class="sr-only">Search games</label>
        <input id="q" type="search" name="@FacetKeys.Text" value="@Filter.Text" placeholder="search games" />

        <label class="check">
            <input type="checkbox" name="@FacetKeys.Archived" value="true" checked="@Filter.IncludeArchived" />
            include archived
        </label>

        <button type="submit">show</button>
    </fieldset>

    @if (Facets.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="facet-grid">
            @foreach (var group in Facets)
            {
                @if (group.Kind is FacetKind.Choice)
                {
                    <div class="facet">
                        <label for="facet-@group.Key">
                            @FacetWords.Group(group.Key)
                            <span class="evidence @Evidence(group)">@FacetWords.Evidence(group.Evidence)</span>
                        </label>
                        <select id="facet-@group.Key" name="@group.Key">
                            @* Always first, always empty: a facet you cannot un-choose is a trap. *@
                            <option value="">any (@group.Total)</option>
                            @foreach (var value in group.Values)
                            {
                                <option value="@value.Token" selected="@value.IsSelected">
                                    @FacetWords.Value(group.Key, value) (@value.Count)
                                </option>
                            }
                        </select>
                    </div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <fieldset class="facet presence">
                        <legend>
                            @FacetWords.Group(group.Key)
                            <span class="evidence @Evidence(group)">@FacetWords.Evidence(group.Evidence)</span>
                        </legend>
                        @foreach (var value in group.Values)
                        {
                            <label class="check">
                                <input type="checkbox" name="@group.Key" value="@value.Token"
                                       checked="@value.IsSelected" />
                                @FacetWords.Value(group.Key, value) <span class="count">@value.Count</span>
                            </label>
                        }
                    </fieldset>
                }
            }
        </div>
    }

    @*
        Said here, in the markup, and not only in a comment. Two of the three sentences are the whole
        reason this panel is shaped the way it is, and a reader ticking boxes is exactly the person
        who would otherwise read an unticked box as a "no".
    *@
    <p class="facet-note">
        Each count is what that choice returns, computed from the same query as the list below.
        A tick box asks which games were <em>seen</em> offering something, so leaving one unticked
        never means a game lacks it — we may simply never have measured it. Where a facet has no
        value for a game it says so in its own words (<em>not identified</em>, <em>not declared</em>,
        <em>nothing negotiated</em>); none of those is a no, and none is folded into another value.
        Open-ended facets offer their @FacetedSearch.MaxValues commonest values; the rest are
        reachable by search and by URL.
    </p>
</form>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<FacetGroup> Facets { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public GameFilter Filter { get; set; } = new();

    private static string Evidence(FacetGroup group) =>
        group.Evidence is FacetEvidence.Measured ? "measured" : "declared";
}
